<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Animals in Our Waters – Lesson # 5</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Ocean Life and Conservation</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Animals in Our Waters</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Participate in conflict resolution strategies with peers. Concept: Simplify complex concepts for group comprehension.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can help resolve disagreements on marine conservation ideas by encouraging active listening and respectful turns (e.g., suggests that each student listens fully before responding.)<br>Can simplify ocean ecosystem concepts, using visuals and gestures (e.g., uses hand motions to show how coral reefs provide homes for fish.)<br>Can clarify marine terms in both English and a familiar language for better understanding (e.g., explains "ecosystem" in simple words and both languages if needed.)</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
    <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 54%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
    <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 13%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing different marine scenes: a vibrant coral reef, a vast open ocean, an endangered sea turtle, and a polluted shoreline. Students discuss in pairs which image they find most impactful and why, using simple sentences. The teacher encourages use of vocabulary like "healthy," "fragile," "polluted," and "endangered."</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to simplify a complex term. For example, "An 'ecosystem' is a big word. It means all the animals, plants, and water in one place working together, like a home for everything. Like our coral reefs, they are an ecosystem." The teacher also models phrases for conflict resolution: "Let's listen to both ideas," "How can we make this clear for everyone?" and pronunciation of "ecosystem" (stress on the first syllable) and /iː/ in "reef."</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean to 'simplify' a concept? Give an example." (Expected answer: To explain something in easy words for everyone to understand.)</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When someone says, 'Let's listen to both ideas,' what is that person trying to do?" (Expected answer: To help people agree or understand each other, to resolve a disagreement.)</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
    </td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation."</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short online article or infographic titled "The Challenge of Plastic Pollution in Our Coastal Areas." The material includes statistics, images of marine animals impacted by plastic, and two opposing viewpoints from fictitious experts on the best conservation actions (e.g., "We should focus on global bans" vs. "We need local community clean-ups"). The text uses vocabulary like "pollution," "habitat," "coastal areas," "species," and "conserve."</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participate in conflict resolution strategies with peers; Simplify complex concepts for group comprehension.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text silently.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main problem discussed in the article?"</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students mark statements about the article as True or False (e.g., "All experts agree on one conservation method." "Plastic pollution only affects big fish.").</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match each expert's proposed conservation action to their name.</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
    </td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using modals for suggestions ("We should protect," "We need to conserve") and present simple/continuous to describe ocean life, while focusing on mediation phrases and clarifying terms (e.g., "What she means is...", "Another way to say that is..."). Pronunciation focus on stress in "biodiversity" and "conservation."</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice: Sentence completion (Mediation Phrases).</b> Students complete sentences using provided mediation phrases and target vocabulary. E.g., "To help understand 'biodiversity,' we _______ (should explain / need to simplify) what makes marine life so diverse." "When they say 'protected zones,' they _______ (are talking about / want to create) special areas where animals can survive."</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled: Rephrasing Challenge.</b> In pairs, Student A says a complex marine term or concept (e.g., "ocean currents," "plankton's role in the food chain"). Student B must rephrase it simply using modals for suggestions and descriptive language. (e.g., "Ocean currents are like big rivers in the sea; they should be understood because they move water and animals.") The teacher encourages using gestures as modeled earlier.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction: Resolving mini-disagreements.</b> Students work in groups of three. Student A states a conservation idea (e.g., "We should only ban plastic bags."). Student B proposes a different idea ("We need to clean up the existing pollution first."). Student C mediates the mini-disagreement using phrases like, "Let's listen to both ideas," and "Maybe we can combine these actions. We should ban bags AND clean coastal areas." The teacher circulates, providing feedback on language use and mediation strategies.</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
    </td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation."</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format: Marine Life Photo Essay Project Clarification Session.</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group has a draft section of their "Marine Life Photo Essay" project, focusing on a specific aspect (e.g., "Coral Reef Ecosystems," "Endangered Marine Animals," "Coastal Conservation Efforts"). One student presents a complex or potentially confusing concept from their photo essay draft (e.g., "the symbiotic relationship between anemones and clownfish" or "the impact of ocean currents on plankton distribution"). Another student in the group acts as a mediator, simplifying the concept for the rest of the group, using visuals from the essay and gestures. The mediator also encourages discussion and helps resolve any minor disagreements about how to best describe or explain a specific marine animal or conservation action in their photo essay, ensuring everyone understands and agrees.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in describing marine life and conservation, collaborative interaction, and effective mediation to simplify concepts and resolve minor group disagreements related to the photo essay content. Students utilize present simple/continuous to describe elements of their essay, and modals to propose solutions or explanations.</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
    </td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes groups during Stage 4, focusing on the mediator's ability to simplify concepts and resolve disagreements. As an exit ticket, each student writes down one complex marine term from their photo essay and then simplifies it into one or two sentences, explaining it clearly to a hypothetical peer. They also write one sentence using a modal for a conservation suggestion related to their photo essay.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and simplicity of the mediated explanation.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of relevant target vocabulary (e.g., "species," "habitat," "conservation," "fragile").</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially stress in multi-syllabic words like "environment" and "biodiversity."</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modals for suggestions ("We should protect...").</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated ability to encourage active listening and respectful turns during group interaction.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one marine concept you helped simplify today. How did you make it easier to understand?"</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned about resolving disagreements in a group discussion?"</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
    </td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reflect on your role as a 'mediator' today. What was easy, and what was challenging when trying to help your peers understand complex ideas or agree on a point?"</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How will practicing mediation skills help you not only in English class but also in other school projects or daily life?"</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in promoting mediation skills? (e.g., group work structure, specific activities, target language use).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for future mediation-focused lessons or when addressing similar challenges? (e.g., more direct modeling of phrases, more varied scenarios for conflict resolution).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which students struggled with simplifying concepts or resolving disagreements and why? What scaffolds can I provide?</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "The Animals in Our Waters" and the "Marine Life Photo Essay" project. The next sequence will introduce a new theme, applying similar 21st-century skills with new content, building on communication and collaboration.</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>